  "final_text": "Both items exist: the dashboard \"EFF-8312d582 Web logs overview\" with four panels, and the alert rule \"EFF-8312d582 5xx spike\", which is enabled.\n\n**Data findings**\n- The data view has request fields (`response`, `clientip`, `geo.dest`, `url`, `bytes`, and others) and a `@timestamp` time field.\n- Only three status codes appear: 200 (12,832 docs), 404 (801), and 503 (441). The only 5xx code is 503.\n- The timestamps run from October 4 to December 3, 2026, which is ahead of today's date (2026-10-12). Because of that, the rule's last-5-minute window may contain no sample data, so the rule may stay quiet unless new data arrives.\n\n**Dashboard panels**\n1. Total requests (count, metric)\n2. Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`, metric)\n3. Requests over time (count per date histogram, area)\n4. Responses by status code (count split by `response.keyword`, pie)\n\nThe dashboard uses a default time range of the last 7 days.\n\n**Alert rule**\n- Type: `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `@timestamp`.\n- Query: `response.keyword` starts with `5`.\n- Condition: count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window.\n- Schedule: every 1 minute.\n- Status: enabled. Alerting health reports `ok`, with a permanent encryption key configured.\n\nThe rule's status still reads `pending` because it has not run yet. I did not check whether it has fired.\n\nTwo choices I made: I used a prefix match on `5` to cover all 5xx codes rather than listing them, and the dashboard has no filters.",
